{"text": "Professional Resume\nA.\n| BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH\nName: Position:\nTristan Seth Kimbrell Assistant Professor of Biology\n\nYear\nDegree arteired Field of study\nTemple University J.D. 2011 Law\nPhiladelphia, PA\nUniversity of Florida Ph.D. 2007 Zoology\nGainesville, FL\nKansas State University B.S. 1999 Biology\nManhattan, KS\nB. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCES DATE\nSouthern University at New Orleans, New Orleans, LA\nAssistant Professor of Biology 08/11/2014 \u2014 Present\nEnvironmental Protection Agency \u2014 Philadelphia, PA \u2014 Law Clerk 05/2010 \u2014- 08/2010\nPennsylvania Superior Court, Philadelphia, PA \u2014 Law Clerk 05/2009 \u2014 08/2009\n\nUniversity of Miami, Coral Gables, FL\n\n\u2014- =\n\nC. MAJOR RESEARCH INTERESTS\nMy primary research interest revolves around exploring the interaction between ecology and the law. |\nam curious to discover how federal environmental laws positively and negatively influence species and\necosystems. For many environmental laws, there has been relatively little scientific research into how\nthey impact the environments they regulate. As a secondary interest, | am also curious to explore issues\nof environmental justice. Environmental degradation tends to disproportionately affect poor\ncommunities, and | want to study how altered ecological dynamics contribute to the burden felt by the\npoor. Finally, | am interested in biological mechanisms that tend to promote or constrain species\nevolution in degraded habitats.\n\nD. PUBLICATIONS (Short list)\n1. Kimbrell, T. 2010. Moving species and non-moving reserves: conservation banking and the impact of\nglobal climate change. Fordham Environmental Law Review 22:119-157.\n2. Kimbrell, T. 2010. Canalization and adaption in a landscape of sources and sinks. Evolutionary\nEcology 24:891 909.\n3. Potts, M. D. and T. Kimbrell. 2010. Rules of thumb for the control of vector-borne diseases ina\nspatial environment. In: Spatial Ecology, eds. S. Cantrell, C. Cosner, and S. Ruan. Chapman and\nHall/CRC\n4. Worman, C. and T. Kimbrell. 2008. Getting to the hart of the matter: did antlers truly cause the\nextinction of the Irish elk? Oikos 117:1397-1405.",
